The internals of `ca::heap` and `ca::arena`.

### Heap

- `heap_classes.hpp`: the 48 size classes, 16 to 128 bytes by 16, then four per doubling up to
  `heap_largest_class` (128 KiB). Every class is a multiple of 16. `heap_class_of(size)` is a shift
  for small sizes and a `bit_width` above 128, always inlined: it sits on the allocation fast path.
- `heap_span.hpp`: a span is 1 MiB aligned to its size, serving one class from its start, its
  header in the last bytes, so `heap_span_of(block)` finds it by masking the block's address. The
  header's first cache line belongs to the owning thread (its free list, its use count); the
  second takes frees from other threads, as an atomic list, or the marker `heap_span_full` once
  the owner found the span exhausted, after which such frees go to the owner heap's `_pending`
  list instead, so a foreign thread never touches a span the owner may release.
- `heap_state.hpp`: one heap per thread (`heap_current`, `constinit thread_local`), holding per
  class a queue of spans with room. Threads that have not allocated yet point at
  `heap_empty_state`, whose classes all hold `heap_empty_span`, so the fast path has no null
  check: the first allocation takes the slow path and builds a real heap. Heaps are never freed: a
  thread's exit hands its heap to a pool (via a thread key, layer 3), and the next new thread
  adopts it with its spans.

### Arena

`arena_state.hpp`: an arena's state lives in its first chunk, right after the `arena_chunk`
header (previous chunk, size), so it never moves and the `arena_allocator`s, which point to it,
stay valid when the `ca::arena` object itself is moved. Blocks are carved from `_top` up to
`_end` of the newest chunk; a request that does not fit starts a new chunk, or gets a chunk of
its own when larger than the chunk size.
